# `bake` pipeline

Runtime physics checkpoint restoration rebinds portable source leases by their exact source/settings fingerprint and stable object identities. This preserves authored ownership separately from live pose/velocity state and native cooking artifacts. Source lease bounds include current physical transforms during replacement; navigation bake clearance remains a host quarantine obligation. These recovery/publication APIs do not change `golem-bake` output or Scribe manifest ownership.

Optional entity-var `physics:` metadata binds ordinary explicit scalar fields to accepted server physics state. Trace these tags through schema resolution, Go writer generation and Scribe attributes; retain identical protobuf/JS/C# codecs and snapshot fingerprints with or without binding metadata. All build outputs and consumer caches for this workstream stay under the physics validation workspace. `qualification/PhysicsUnity.ps1` owns repeatable staged Unity fixture setup and reference capture; it never edits a game repository or installs an Editor implicitly.

Portable physics source is a separate versioned JSON authoring contract, not entity/world YAML or native backend cooking. Unity captures resolved body-local geometry and metadata; the Go physics content layer validates/cooks it separately. Keep the Scribe exact ownership signature and manifest-last source transaction intact. Canonical small cross-language JSON fixtures are committed; generated Unity projects, observations and caches remain under `D:/Demiurgos/tests/physics-validation`.

Physics `content.Prepared` owns bounded immutable source/policy captures and transactional world construction. Backend-specific `box3d.CookShape` owns private hull/BVH acceleration and versioned native-image artifacts. These APIs are independent of `golem-bake` and footprint v1. Hash portable source, explicit conversion/cooking settings and backend snapshot separately; no local workspace override or dirty-source test qualifies published dependency resolution.

`golem-bake` remains the schema/code-generation CLI. Explicit server navigation geometry is processed by the separate navmesh adapter module's `navbuild` package and `golem-nav-bake` command, which delegate pure-Go baking to navmesh-go. Its bounded `{Generator?, Scene, Config}` JSON is not entity/world schema YAML and is not inferred from collision footprints. Do not add a native navigation build subprocess to codegen. Optional Scribe export is authoring-only; preserve its managed artifact planning and transaction ownership.

Navigation's `bake.PreparedSource` is an opaque immutable geometry capture bound to exact origin bits and strictness. It owns resolved data and a lazy, concurrency-safe spatial index; repeated builds still validate configuration, precision, limits and artifacts. Dynamic worlds publish the desired source/capsule/revision coherently, while workers retain their captured source through completion. Returned diagnostics and link/profile arrays must remain independently owned. Account for retained source/index memory separately from managed snapshots; neither snapshots nor prepared source captures may keep the other alive. These navigation captures are distinct from schema/codegen resolution and do not change CLI artifact ownership.

Reusable qualification geometry is generated by navmesh-go, with the actual server workload in the separate `examples/navigation-stress` module. Qualification scripts stage source copies and candidate archives under the validation workspace. Generated large worlds, executables, caches and measurements are evidence, not schema/codegen inputs or committed fixtures; canonical small configuration and authoring fixtures remain ordinary Git files.

The separate `examples/physics-stress` consumer constructs deterministic synthetic combined-load geometry and bakes its matching plane through navmesh-go. Its full resident triangle count is unique cooked geometry, not multiplied instance counts. Keep its fixed full workloads and explicit reduced diagnostics distinct; both scene hashes and exact source snapshots belong with generated evidence. It preserves the independent existing navigation qualification gates.

When a schema-driven feature changes, trace it through schema loading, validation, template DTOs, proto generation, Go generation, JS generation, and any affected templates.

At the envelope validation stage, `Bake` calls `ResolveProtoData` once and passes that result to proto rendering and integrations. Catalog metadata is already resolved by world construction; file-content validation remains a separate pre-output step. Consumers must not repeat schema resolution or tag allocation. Keep integration output ownership, manager lifecycle, framework adapters and client runtime changes separate from schema/codec refactors.

The integration handoff consists of ordered envelope variants/tags/roles, ordered entity state/delta fields with presence/visibility/dirty-bit metadata, payload fields plus targeting metadata, collection/custom-type dependencies and catalog item/key metadata. These are read-only after resolution. Integration decoration uses per-target entity copies; output selection and defaults live in `codegen/integration_outputs.go`, not schema descriptors. Legacy flat DTOs remain supported by explicit adapters, not alternate emitter policy.

If a new field, target, branch, helper, import, or generated method is added in one target, check the parallel Go, JS, proto, and template paths for the same behavior.

Prefer focused regression tests that inspect generated output for emitted names, imports, helper functions, method shapes, tags, and format strings; do not rely only on helper-level logic tests.

Entity, command, and event root `tag` values pin fields in their protocol oneof envelopes. Untagged declarations keep the legacy sequential assignment, explicit declarations are additive, and `EntityRemoved` stays immediately after the untagged entity pairs. Validate protobuf field legality and all implicit/explicit overlaps before rendering; never repair envelope tags in generated output.

Command and event payload fields may retain legacy alphabetical numbering, or every field in one command/event may use `{ tag: N, type: T }` to pin its direct protobuf field number. Never mix tagged and untagged fields in one payload; entity-targeted commands/events reserve tag 1 for `entity_id`.

Be careful with nested formatting in generators: code that emits `fmt` strings or TS/Go source can need a different level of escaping than normal application code.

After schema or codegen changes that affect bake output, verify `go build ./cmd/golem-bake` and targeted generator tests. For cross-target changes, confirm both Go and JS generated outputs still compile.

For shared codec changes, also compile/run generated C# against the standalone Unity codec sources, exercise generated Go full/public state behavior, and distinguish semantic equivalence, exact bytes and deterministic generated source. Do not impose sorted Go map wire order. Run representative generated consumers in temporary directories; a skipped required consumer check is not acceptance evidence. Preserve exact baseline fingerprints even where their current coverage is incomplete.

Retire a configured shared-only bridge with `golem-bake --remove-integration <target>` before removing its integration stanza. Removal checks generated headers and confines known outputs to the project; it never deletes authored adapters or recursively removes an output directory.

The `threlte` target emits raw `GolemThrelte.ts` compiled by the consumer's Svelte toolchain. It requires `js-client`, an explicit forward-slash module-prefix `protocol_import` (normalize a missing trailing `/`), and an output directory outside `js-client` output (whose finalizer recursively compiles TypeScript). Validate these requirements before writing any bake output. Keep empty entity/world/event sections valid in both generated JS and the Threlte adapter.

Framework consumer typing gates cover Phaser and Threlte full, empty, global-only, world-only and entity-only schemas. `GOLEM_REQUIRE_CONSUMERS=1` also makes their missing dependencies fail. Newly generated event managers require runtime 0.3.2; regenerate JS and framework integrations together. Existing generated outputs remain usable with the updated runtime.
